# ~/.screenrc
#
# vim motions for moving between split regions -- C-a h/j/k/l walks left,
# down, up, right, the way the hands already do it everywhere else. this
# is the binding screen's own man page suggests, under `focus'.
#
# regions, not windows. C-a S and C-a | make the splits; C-a n/p still
# cycle windows *inside* whichever region has focus, untouched.
#
# all four are for real splits only: moving left from the leftmost region
# does nothing rather than wrapping, so on an unsplit screen every one of
# these is a no-op. that's screen's behaviour, not a broken binding.
#
# h/j/l cost nothing -- j was unbound, and l's old job (redisplay) already
# had C-a C-l. the two that were somebody's only key:
#
#   C-a h    was hardcopy -> moves down to C-a C-h, which was only ever a
#                            fourth way to say prev (backspace, p and C-p
#                            all still do that)
#   C-a k    was kill     -> stays on C-a C-k, which was always its twin.
#                            no loss, and one fewer way to destroy a
#                            window by fumbling a motion key

bind h focus left
bind j focus down
bind k focus up
bind l focus right

bind ^h hardcopy

# dead windows stay put instead of vanishing the instant their program exits.
# a finished command leaves its output sitting there as an artifact you can
# read at your leisure; `q' destroys it, `r' would resurrect it.
#
# BOTH KEYS ARE BARE, pressed inside the dead window itself -- there is no C-a
# prefix on either, and no prefixed spelling exists. (C-a r is `wrap', and
# always was. it is the obvious guess and it is wrong.)
#
# q rather than screen's customary k, for quit. nothing collides either way:
# zombie keys never go through C-a, so `bind k focus up' above is untouched.
#
# this is also what stops a finished window's region being backfilled. screen
# only swaps another window into a region when the region goes *empty*, and a
# zombie never empties it -- so the window that was there stays there, which
# is the whole thing anyone wanted. anything walking the regions to remove
# them by hand is solving a problem this line prevents.
#
# resurrect does not work on commando's windows, and that is not a fault here:
# ~/src/commando hands each window a throwaway script from its spool dir which
# ends in `rm -f "$SELF"', so by the time the window is dead there is nothing
# left on disk for screen to relaunch. use q on those.
zombie qr
